1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,900
Four o'clock on the afternoon of December 31.

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:08,510
Two sheets of paper sit side by side on the director's desk at a mid-sized equipment distributor.

3
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:20,260
On the left lies the closed year-end income statement: 10 billion VND in sales revenue, 6.5 billion in cost of goods sold, and 1.5 billion in operating overhead, leaving a clean 2 billion VND net profit.

4
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:26,740
On the right glows the corporate bank screen for Account 112: available checking cash reads just 50 million VND.

5
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:32,820
In sixty minutes, at five o'clock sharp, 500 million VND in December salaries must hit employee accounts.

6
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:34,790
The firm is 450 million VND short.

7
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:35,890
Nobody stole money.

8
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:37,920
Nobody added a single column wrong.

9
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:42,540
Where did 2 billion in book profit, plus 1 billion in opening cash, actually go?

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,200
To track down that missing cash, rewind to the start of the fourth quarter and follow every wire leaving the bank.

11
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,610
Entering the quarter, Account 112 held 1 billion VND in starting working capital.

12
00:00:54,610 --> 00:01:03,420
Across the quarter, funded alongside incoming customer installment collections, the company wired 7.45 billion VND in hard cash straight to merchandise suppliers.

13
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:13,590
Here is how those 7.45 billion VND split up: 6.5 billion paid for stock earmarked to fulfill a large wholesale contract, and 950 million bought extra buffer inventory for early next year.

14
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:17,560
Accounting debits all 7.45 billion to Account 156, Merchandise Inventory.

15
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:24,640
Real money left the bank immediately, yet the income statement recorded zero expense while those cartons sat on warehouse shelves.

16
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:27,670
Move forward to November 15.

17
00:01:27,670 --> 00:01:36,760
The warehouse team ships all 10 billion VND of equipment to the wholesale buyer, and both sides sign the delivery handover certificate and VAT invoice.

18
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:53,320
Under accrual accounting rules in Circular 99/2025/TT-BTC, delivering control of the goods obligates finance to book the entire 10 billion VND as sales revenue on Account 511 immediately, while transferring 6.5 billion out of inventory into Cost of Goods Sold on Account 632.

19
00:01:53,320 --> 00:02:02,780
Check the live corporate bank statement, however, and a different reality shows up: the wholesale client wired only 7.5 billion VND into Account 112 upfront.

20
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:11,210
Because the contract includes 60-to-90-day credit terms, the remaining 2.5 billion VND sits on Account 131 as an unpaid customer receivable.

21
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:18,910
On December 25, the accounting department closes the quarterly books for the year-end financial report.

22
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:32,700
Operating overhead across the quarter totaled 1.5 billion VND: 1 billion already wired for warehouse lease, freight, and utilities, plus 500 million owed for December staff salaries sitting unpaid on Account 334.

23
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:44,210
Look at the income statement arithmetic now: 10 billion VND in gross sales revenue, minus 6.5 billion in cost of goods sold, minus 1.5 billion in operating and payroll expenses.

24
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:48,700
Bottom-line net profit prints precisely 2 billion VND on Account 421.

25
00:02:48,700 --> 00:03:01,180
Meanwhile, the spare 950 million VND in extra merchandise has not been sold yet, so it never enters the expense ledger at all and remains parked cleanly on the balance sheet under Account 156.

26
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:13,070
Now push that 2 billion profit sheet to the corner of the desk and count pure bank cash moving through Account 112 from October through December 31 at two o'clock.

27
00:03:13,070 --> 00:03:22,710
Cash flowing into the account totaled 1 billion starting cash plus 7.5 billion wired by the wholesale client, making 8.5 billion VND in inflows.

28
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:32,550
Cash flowing out totaled 7.45 billion wired to merchandise suppliers plus 1 billion paid for rent and freight, making 8.45 billion VND in outflows.

29
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:38,510
Subtract 8.45 billion out from 8.5 billion in, and Account 112 holds just 50 million VND.

30
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:47,020
Against a 500 million VND payroll sheet due in a few hours, the corporate safe faces an immediate 450 million VND cash deficit.

31
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:51,570
Did any of that missing liquidity evaporate into thin air?

32
00:03:51,570 --> 00:03:58,720
Not a single dong disappeared—every bit of 3.45 billion VND sits right inside the balance sheet in two physical places.

33
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:04,310
First, 2.5 billion VND sits on signed customer invoices under Account 131, Trade Receivables.

34
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:14,350
You delivered real hardware and booked full profit on that sale, yet in cash terms you handed a major wholesale client a 2.5 billion zero-interest loan for two months.

35
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:21,800
Second, 950 million VND sits inside cardboard cartons stacked across steel pallets under Account 156, Merchandise Inventory.

36
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:30,030
Unfortunately, neither an unpaid customer invoice nor unsold stock sitting in the warehouse can clear employee paychecks at five o'clock.

37
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:38,490
To connect those two conflicting financial pages, finance builds the Indirect Statement of Cash Flows.

38
00:04:38,490 --> 00:04:42,640
Start at +2 billion VND in reported accounting net profit.

39
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:53,460
Next, subtract the 2.5 billion VND tied up in unpaid Account 131 customer receivables and the 950 million VND added to Account 156 warehouse inventory.

40
00:04:53,460 --> 00:05:01,620
Then add back the 500 million VND in December payroll already deducted on the P&L yet still unpaid on Account 334.

41
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:08,210
Add those lines together and Operating Cash Flow drops straight to negative 950 million VND.

42
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:18,810
Take 1 billion VND opening cash minus 950 million burned in working capital, and ending cash on Account 112 equals 50 million VND to the exact dong.

43
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:24,940
By four thirty on December 31, only thirty minutes remain before banks close for the holiday.

44
00:05:24,940 --> 00:05:36,660
To ensure fifty staff members get paid on schedule, the director calls the commercial bank to draw 450 million VND on an emergency short-term credit line under Account 341—carrying an illustrative 10.5% annual interest rate.

45
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:43,720
Combining that 450 million loan wire with the last 50 million cash on hand clears all 500 million VND in December wages on Account 334.

46
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:55,440
Pause and weigh that trade: a profitable company reporting 2 billion VND in annual earnings just borrowed 450 million at 10.5% interest simply to cover the 2.5 billion zero-interest credit window it extended to one customer.

47
00:05:56,890 --> 00:06:04,400
This year-end scramble reveals why fast-growing profitable companies run out of cash during boom quarters—a trap known in finance as overtrading.

48
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:12,070
Everything traces back to the Cash Conversion Cycle, CCC: the number of days cash sits tied up between wiring suppliers and getting paid by clients.

49
00:06:12,070 --> 00:06:23,830
When you pay suppliers upfront while keeping 950 million in spare warehouse stock and handing B2B clients 60-to-90-day credit, each new 10 billion wholesale order consumes far more cash upfront than it returns in the near term.

50
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:31,240
Double your sales pace on those exact terms without speeding up collections or tightening stock, and your bank balance hits zero twice as fast.

51
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:38,850
At four o'clock, neither sheet on that desk is lying—each one just tracks a completely different clock.

52
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:44,320
The Income Statement confirms you priced contracts above cost and ran a profitable quarter.

53
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:52,430
Only your bank statement and Statement of Cash Flows prove whether cash actually cleared in time to meet rent, suppliers, and salaries.

54
00:06:52,430 --> 00:07:05,650
Every month, read Net Profit on Account 421 side by side with Operating Cash Flow, shorten collection windows on Account 131 with milestone billing or early-payment discounts, and keep Account 156 warehouse shelves lean.

55
00:07:05,650 --> 00:07:10,880
Book profit impresses the annual report; real cash on Account 112 keeps payroll moving.
